---
import '../styles/nest.css';

// BASE_URL is "/actions-demo" (no trailing slash) under this config.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Registry Publish Key</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Create a publish-only CI key for the Stellar Registry with your nido wallet: the key can publish new versions of your wasm and nothing else."
    />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={`${base}favicon.svg`} />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Fraunces:ital,opsz,wght@0,9..144,600;0,9..144,800;1,9..144,800&family=Hanken+Grotesk:wght@400..800&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400..600&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="halo" style="width:520px;height:520px;top:-220px;right:-160px"></div>
    <div class="halo" style="width:420px;height:420px;bottom:-200px;left:-180px;opacity:.3"></div>
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="topbar">
        <span class="wordmark">actions<b>·</b>demo</span>
        <nav>
          <span class="chip warn"><span class="dot"></span>testnet</span>
          <a class="chip" href="https://github.com/stellar-registry/actions-demo">GitHub</a>
        </nav>
      </header>

      <section class="hero rise">
        <span class="chip acc">Stellar Registry · nido</span>
        <h1>A CI key that can <em>only</em> publish.</h1>
        <p>
          Your nido wallet adds a new key to its policy document. The key may call
          <code>publish</code> on one registry, for the wasm names you list, with your
          account as the author. It can't publish anything
          else, and it has no say over the account's funds or policy. Put its secret in GitHub
          Actions and releases publish themselves.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 1 · registry -->
      <section class="step" id="step-registry" data-state="active">
        <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">1</span><h2>Registry</h2><span class="chip good hidden" id="registry-ok">checked</span></div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>The registry the key may publish to, and the wasm names it may publish.</p>
          <div class="field">
            <label for="registry">Registry contract</label>
            <input class="input mono" id="registry" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" />
            <div class="hint">Defaults to the testnet <em>unverified</em> registry, which has no manager: the first publish of a name claims it.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="field">
            <label for="names">Wasm names</label>
            <input class="input mono" id="names" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" />
            <div class="hint">Comma-separated. The release workflow publishes the crate name, <code>actions-demo</code>.</div>
          </div>
          <label class="check">
            <input type="checkbox" id="allow-hash" />
            <span>
              Also allow <code>publish_hash</code>. Only needed for a wasm over 60 KiB, which is
              too big to <code>publish</code> in one transaction; the workflow then uploads it
              first and binds its hash.
            </span>
          </label>
          <div class="row"><button class="btn acc" id="check-registry">Check registry</button></div>
          <dl class="facts hidden" id="registry-facts" style="margin-top:16px"></dl>
          <p class="status" id="registry-status"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 2 · wallet -->
      <section class="step" id="step-wallet" data-state="locked">
        <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">2</span><h2>nido wallet</h2><span class="chip good hidden" id="wallet-ok">connected</span></div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>
            The smart account that will be the wasm's author. Its passkey approves the policy
            change in a nido popup.
          </p>
          <div class="row">
            <button class="btn acc" id="connect">Connect nido</button>
            <a class="btn soft" id="new-nido" target="_blank" rel="noopener">No account? Create one</a>
          </div>
          <details style="margin-top:12px">
            <summary>Wallet domain</summary>
            <div class="field" style="margin-top:10px">
              <input class="input mono" id="nido-base" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" />
              <div class="hint">The hosted testnet wallet is <code>nido.fyi</code>. Use <code>http://localhost:4321</code> for a local nido.</div>
            </div>
          </details>
          <dl class="facts hidden" id="wallet-facts" style="margin-top:16px"></dl>
          <div class="alert warn hidden" id="first-apply">
            <strong>This account has never applied a policy document.</strong> <code>apply_doc</code>
            replaces the account's starting rule with the document. Afterwards your passkey keeps
            admin rights over the account through the document's <code>admin</code> rule, the same
            baseline nido's own policy pages use.
          </div>
          <div id="current-rules"></div>
          <p class="status" id="wallet-status"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 3 · key -->
      <section class="step" id="step-key" data-state="locked">
        <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">3</span><h2>CI publish key</h2><span class="chip good hidden" id="key-ok">stored</span></div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>
            An ordinary Stellar ed25519 key, generated in this browser. It signs the publish and
            pays its fees, so the page funds it with test XLM.
          </p>
          <div class="row" id="gen-row"><button class="btn acc" id="generate">Generate key</button></div>
          <div class="hidden" id="secret-box">
            <div class="alert danger">
              <strong>This is the only time the secret is shown.</strong> Copy it into a GitHub
              Actions secret named <code>CI_PUBLISH_SECRET_KEY</code> now. This page never
              stores it, and closing or reloading the page loses it.
            </div>
            <div class="secret">
              <div class="label">Secret key · CI_PUBLISH_SECRET_KEY</div>
              <div class="value" id="secret-value"></div>
              <button class="btn sm soft" id="copy-secret">Copy secret</button>
            </div>
            <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="stored" /> I stored the secret in GitHub. Hide it.</label>
          </div>
          <dl class="facts hidden" id="key-facts" style="margin-top:4px"></dl>
          <p class="status" id="key-status"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 4 · review + apply -->
      <section class="step" id="step-apply" data-state="locked">
        <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">4</span><h2>Review and apply</h2><span class="chip good hidden" id="apply-ok">applied</span></div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>
            The updated policy document, compared with the one the account has now. The nido
            window then shows the <code>apply_doc</code> transaction and asks for your passkey.
          </p>
          <div id="diff"></div>
          <dl class="facts" id="doc-facts"></dl>
          <details>
            <summary>Full document (canonical JSON)</summary>
            <pre class="doc" id="doc-json"></pre>
          </details>
          <div class="row" style="margin-top:16px"><button class="btn acc" id="apply">Approve in nido</button></div>
          <p class="status" id="apply-status"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 5 · result -->
      <section class="step" id="step-done" data-state="locked">
        <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">5</span><h2>Wire up GitHub</h2></div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>The rule as it now sits on-chain, and the repo settings the release workflow reads.</p>
          <dl class="facts" id="done-facts"></dl>
          <div class="section-label">Repository settings</div>
          <table class="settings">
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Kind</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody id="settings"></tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="section-label">Or with the GitHub CLI</div>
          <pre class="doc" id="gh-commands"></pre>
          <p class="status">
            Then merge changes to <code>main</code> as usual. The release workflow keeps one
            <code>chore: release</code> PR open with the next version and changelog; merging that
            PR tags, builds, attests and publishes it. A contract's first version is released by
            running the Release workflow by hand on <code>main</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="explain">
        <h2>How the pieces fit</h2>
        <p>
          The release workflow uses the shared
          <a href="https://github.com/stellar-registry/actions">stellar-registry/actions</a>
          workflows at <code>main</code>:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>release-pr</strong> proposes the next version from conventional commits.</li>
          <li><strong>detect-releases</strong> tags <code>actions-demo-v&lt;version&gt;</code> when that version lands on main.</li>
          <li><strong>contract-release</strong> builds with <code>stellar scaffold build</code>, creates the GitHub release, and signs build provenance.</li>
          <li><strong>registry-publish</strong> verifies the provenance and the wasm's <code>binver</code>, then calls <code>publish</code> with the wasm as your account, signed by this key. A wasm over 60 KiB is uploaded first and bound with <code>publish_hash</code> instead.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The key is registered as <code>External(ed25519 verifier, public key)</code> on a rule
          scoped to the registry. perch's interpreter policy on that rule checks every call: the
          function must be <code>publish</code> (or <code>publish_hash</code>, if you allowed it),
          argument 0 (the name) must be one you listed,
          and argument 1 (the author) must be the account itself. If the secret leaks, the worst
          case is someone publishing a version of your listed wasm names with code you didn't
          build. It shows up on-chain without a matching attested GitHub release, and running
          this page again rotates the key out. See the
          <a href="https://github.com/stellar-registry/actions-demo#security-model">security model</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
      <footer>
        Built on <a href="https://nido.fyi">nido</a>, <a href="https://github.com/stellar-registry/perch">perch</a>
        and the <a href="https://github.com/stellar-registry/contracts">Stellar Registry</a>. Testnet only.
      </footer>
    </div>
    <script>
      import '../lib/app';
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
